TU1NZ / WANT ME SEEN — COMMERCIAL S11.2-R12 DIAGNOSIS

Scope: source-only. No runtime start, deployment, database write, evidence
rewrite, provider mutation, or S11 transition was performed.

Observed R11 sequence

- S8 runtime readiness, poller, lease, successful poll, event path, technical
  runtime latency, and S9 health were GREEN.
- S10 health exited non-zero with status 34.
- The bounded S10 safe report contained S10_TELEGRAM_HEALTH_RED.
- The recovery diagnosis instead returned COMMUNITY_CHILD_CODE_UNKNOWN_RED.
- A later independent read-only check was GREEN, which supports only a
  possible transient provider or timing condition.

Root cause

The S10 health wrapper already generated the canonical bounded safe code.
Child loss occurred in two downstream Control paths:

1. The recovery stream parser selected community outer envelopes but ignored
   the standalone S10_TELEGRAM_HEALTH_RED safe report.
2. The systemd health gate normalised child reports only for the S9 community
   exit range and not for S10 exit 34.

Contract decision

- Preserve outer_code S10_2D_COMMUNITY_STATE_RED for compatibility.
- Preserve child_code S10_TELEGRAM_HEALTH_RED exactly.
- Set component TELEGRAM_CHANNEL_HEALTH.
- Classify the observed single RED as TRANSIENT_PROVIDER_OR_TIMING_CANDIDATE.
- Bound the next action to READ_ONLY_RECHECK_BEFORE_NEW_RECOVERY_AUTHORIZATION.
- Never convert this classification into an automatic retry.
- Repeated RED remains a persistent provider blocker.
- Missing and unknown child codes remain fail-closed.
- Keep S9 health independent from the later S10 Telegram channel check.

Privacy

Only bounded technical state codes and aggregate simulator decisions are
retained. No credentials, identifiers, messages, invite material, media,
network addresses, or connection strings are included.
